    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1: Yes.** The search log covers seven days before publication up to now, with current data, and shows only the established pattern before the burst. I am treating the burst as misuse because of the new proxy and fingerprint and the payout changes, though that is not formally confirmed.\n- **A2: Yes.** The check scored the account at 30 minutes, eleven minutes after the burst, and did not flag it. This assumes the check had access to request and account-change data, which the record does not state.\n- **A3: No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is also consistent with one attacker redirecting several compromised accounts to the same place.\n- **A4: Yes, weakly.** Thirty days with no victim report or incident finding is some evidence, but reports can lag, and zero findings may only mean nobody looked.\n- **A5: Yes.** A new origin, a new fingerprint and payout changes right after publication are enough to warrant investigation.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1: No.** The two chargebacks recorded at account X dispute payments that are D's transactions, so D has 2 per 100, the same as A, B and C.\n- **B2: No.** A combined figure is an average and says nothing about each account. With 8 chargebacks and 12 alerts over 400 transactions, any uneven spread of alerts puts at least one account above 5 percent.\n- **B3: Cannot tell.** The log says 3 account sources were searched but not whether that is all of them, and a name search would not catch accounts held through other people.\n- **B4: Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days does not establish independence; the second may have read the first note, and both may rest on the same evidence.\n- **B5: No.** The payment was blocked as suspected fraud, so it never completed and could not have produced a chargeback.\n- **B6: Yes.** Shared templates, a common descriptor pattern, settlement through the same four accounts and the link to a rejected application justify looking at them together.\n\nRESULT CF-024 | A1: Yes | A2: Yes | A3: No, not by itself | A4: Yes, weakly | A5: Yes | B1: No | B2: No | B3: Cannot tell | B4: Cannot tell | B5: No | B6: Yes"
